---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import StatTiles from "../../../components/StatTiles.astro";
import {
  REPO,
  RELEASE_TAG,
  PATCH_TAG,
  RELEASE_URL,
  PATCH_URL,
  RELEASE_CHANGELOG,
  PATCH_CHANGELOG,
  RELEASE_INSTALL_SCRIPT,
  RELEASE_DATE,
  a3bFile,
  a3bPrefill,
  a3bDefaults,
  a3bDecode,
  flashNextRows,
  hotSet,
  flashNextMtp,
  mtp27b,
  halo16k,
  haloContributor,
  otherRows,
  tiers,
  tierKld,
} from "../../../data/release043";
import { flashNext } from "../../../data/release042";

const description =
  "hipfire 0.4.3 and 0.4.3.1 benchmarks: Qwen3.6-35B-A3B prefill on the R9700 (0.4.2.1 vs 0.4.3, mq4r and mq4rs), A3B mq4rs decode 157 to 232 tok/s, Flash-Next prefill on the R9700 and Strix Halo, and MTP decode from the release gates, with fixtures and sources.";

const toc = [
  { href: "#a3b-prefill", label: "A3B prefill" },
  { href: "#a3b-decode", label: "A3B decode (0.4.3.1)" },
  { href: "#flash-next-r9700", label: "Flash-Next on the R9700" },
  { href: "#mtp-decode", label: "MTP decode" },
  { href: "#halo", label: "Strix Halo" },
  { href: "#changes", label: "Other measured changes" },
  { href: "#tiers", label: "New Flash-Next tiers" },
  { href: "#repro", label: "Reproduce it" },
  { href: "#limits", label: "Known limits" },
];

const f0 = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US", { maximumFractionDigits: 0 });
const f1 = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString("en-US", { minimumFractionDigits: 1, maximumFractionDigits: 1 });
const f2 = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
const x = (n: number) => `${n.toFixed(2)}×`;
const pctOf = (a: number, b: number) => `${b >= a ? "+" : "−"}${Math.abs((b / a - 1) * 100).toFixed(1)} %`;
---

<Base title="hipfire 0.4.3 benchmarks" description={description}>
  <main class="mkt bench">
    <header class="bench-hero">
      <div class="mkt-wrap">
        <p class="mkt-eyebrow">Benchmarks · released {RELEASE_DATE}</p>
        <h1 class="bench-h1">hipfire 0.4.3 benchmarks</h1>
        <p class="mkt-intro">
          <a href={RELEASE_URL}>hipfire 0.4.3</a> leads with prefill speed for A3B models on the Radeon AI PRO R9700, with
          the new <code>{a3bFile.mq4rsTag}</code> quant, and speeds up the Flash-Next prefill path on the R9700.
          <a href={PATCH_URL}>hipfire 0.4.3.1</a> moves A3B mq4rs decode onto the PM4 replay route. Every number below
          names its card, fixture, method and date, and links its source. This page covers the 0.4.3 and 0.4.3.1
          releases; the 0.4.2 and 0.4.1 numbers stay on their own pages.
        </p>
        <StatTiles release="0.4.3" linkBase="" />
        <nav class="bench-toc" aria-label="benchmark sections">
          {toc.map((t) => <a href={t.href}>{t.label}</a>)}
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="mkt-wrap">
      <div class="release">
      <section id="a3b-prefill" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Qwen3.6-35B-A3B · prefill on the R9700, 0.4.2.1 vs 0.4.3</h2>
        <p>
          Prefill tok/s on one {a3bPrefill.card}, measured {a3bPrefill.date} in a single timing hold (job
          {" "}{a3bPrefill.job}) with the three arms interleaved. Each cell is the median of 9 samples: 3 fresh
          <code>hipfire bench</code> processes with <code>--runs 3</code>. “0.4.2” here is {a3bPrefill.v042Build}, the latest
          0.4.2 release.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>prefill tok/s</th>
                <th class="num">0.4.2.1 mq4r</th>
                <th class="num">0.4.3 mq4r</th>
                <th class="num">0.4.3 mq4rs</th>
                <th class="num">mq4rs vs 0.4.2.1 mq4r</th>
                <th class="num">engine only (mq4r)</th>
                <th class="num">quant only (0.4.3)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {a3bPrefill.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>pp{r.pp}</td>
                  <td class="num">{f0(r.v042_mq4r)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{f0(r.v043_mq4r)}</td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{f0(r.v043_mq4rs)}</strong></td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{x(r.speedup)}</strong></td>
                  <td class="num">{x(r.engineOnly)}</td>
                  <td class="num">{x(r.quantOnly)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          Command: <code>{a3bPrefill.command}</code>, synthetic token ids. The new
          <code>{a3bFile.mq4rsTag}</code> quant is where most of the gain comes from (3.5× to 5.5× over the same 0.4.3
          engine on the old mq4r file). The 0.4.3 engine alone is 1.0× to 1.8× on mq4r and removes the 0.4.2 long-context
          falloff (0.4.2.1 loses 41 % from pp512 to pp16384; 0.4.3 on mq4r does not fall off). Source:
          {" "}<a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, “Prefill perf comparison”; receipt
          <code>A3B-PREFILL-042-VS-043.md</code>.
        </p>

        <h3>Read this before comparing columns</h3>
        <p>
          Each version ran its default config, and the defaults differ. The 0.4.2.1 → 0.4.3 mq4r column is therefore “what
          a user gets from upgrading without changing the model file”, not a single-variable engine A/B.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>default</th><th>0.4.2.1 mq4r</th><th>0.4.3 mq4r</th><th>0.4.3 mq4rs</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {a3bDefaults.map((d) => (
                <tr><td>{d.item}</td><td>{d.a}</td><td>{d.b}</td><td>{d.c}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          The symmetric IU4 MoE prefill route is only admitted on the mq4rs file. <strong>Fast mq4rs prefill is gfx1201
          only</strong>; on other GPUs the mq4rs file needs <code>HIPFIRE_MOE_SYM_IU4=0</code> (about 5× slower prefill).
          mq4rs file: <a href={a3bFile.hf}>hipfire-models/qwen3.6-35b-a3b</a>, {a3bFile.mq4rsGb} GB, sha256
          <code class="hash">{a3bFile.mq4rsSha256}</code>; the mq4r arms used the registry-pinned file, sha256
          <code class="hash">{a3bFile.mq4rSha256}</code>. WikiText-2 KLD, same harness: {a3bFile.kldMq4rs} for mq4rs against
          {" "}{a3bFile.kldMq4r} for mq4r. Thermals counted against the 0.4.3 arms: the card ran at a lower mean clock
          and a higher junction temperature in the B and C arms than in A (receipt, “Caveats”). Decode was not compared
          here (<code>--tg 1</code>).
        </p>

        <h3>Late prefill steps on mq4rs (pp16000)</h3>
        <p>
          The last lever steps of the cycle, on <code>qwen3.6-35b-a3b-d53.mq4rs</code> only and not part of the table above:
          pp16000 (two chunks, 8192 + 7808 rows), 3 fresh processes per arm, three separate same-session A/Bs.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead><tr><th>step</th><th class="num">before</th><th class="num">after</th><th class="num">change</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {a3bPrefill.levers.map((l, i) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>A/B {i + 1}</td>
                  <td class="num">{f1(l.from)} tok/s</td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{f1(l.to)} tok/s</strong></td>
                  <td class="num">+{l.pct} %</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          The +{a3bPrefill.leverSumPct} % total is the sum of the three, not a single A/B; the card runs a few percent slower
          on some days than others. The routes are exact-gfx1201 defaults, byte-identical to the code they replace:
          KLD is unchanged on mq4rs (the four-corpus KLD sequence is byte-equal at every step). That does not hold for the
          asymmetric mq4r file: its WikiText-2 prefill KLD moves
          {" "}{a3bPrefill.mq4rKldPrefill.before} → {a3bPrefill.mq4rKldPrefill.after} (Δ +{a3bPrefill.mq4rKldPrefill.delta},
          CI95 {a3bPrefill.mq4rKldPrefill.ci}), inside the quality bar. Source:
          <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, “Detail” and Known issues.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="a3b-decode" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Qwen3.6-35B-A3B mq4rs · decode on the R9700, 0.4.3 → 0.4.3.1</h2>
        <p>
          The symmetric-expert A3B file used to decode on the HIP graph. In 0.4.3.1 it takes the automatic PM4 replay
          route on an exact gfx1201 card (one GPU, no drafter), the same default <code>.mq4r</code> already had. Output is
          bit-exact against the HIP route. Nothing to configure; <code>replay.backend = "hip"</code> turns it off.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>decode tok/s · {a3bDecode.card}</th>
                <th class="num">HIP route (0.4.3)</th>
                <th class="num">PM4 replay (0.4.3.1)</th>
                <th class="num">change</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {a3bDecode.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.label}</td>
                  <td class="num">{r.hipTxt}</td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{r.pm4Txt}</strong></td>
                  <td class="num">+{r.pct} %</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          Measured on card B with <code>{a3bDecode.command}</code>, default config for both arms (the HIP arm set with
          <code>replay.backend = "hip"</code>), 3 fresh processes per arm. Greedy ids are identical between the two routes on
          prose, code, copy and 1024-token reasoning prompts, and the replay harness reports logits, KV and recurrent state
          bit-equal. This changes the decode route only; prefill is unaffected (mq4rs prefill stays about 20K tok/s at
          4K–8K prompts, as in 0.4.3). It does not apply to other GPUs. Source:
          <a href={PATCH_URL}>v0.4.3.1 release notes</a>.
        </p>
        <p class="footnote">
          Re-check at the release gate ({a3bDecode.gate.date}, RC <code>{a3bDecode.gate.rc}</code>, 3 fresh processes, same
          command): tg128 ctx 128 <strong>{a3bDecode.gate.ctx128.map(f1).join(" / ")}</strong> tok/s, ctx 8192
          {" "}{a3bDecode.gate.ctx8192.map(f1).join(" / ")}, pp128 {a3bDecode.gate.pp128.map(f0).join(" / ")}. The Redline
          PM4 route is bit-exact against the HIP route (logits hash <code class="hash">{a3bDecode.gate.redlineLogitsHash}</code>
          identical across AQL, HIP and blob). Source: <code>RC-GATES-0.4.3.1-RESULT.md</code> §2.
          <code>--spec mtp</code> now also loads on mq4rs, but the default stays AR and an MTP-loaded request does not use
          PM4 replay.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="flash-next-r9700" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Qwen3.8-Flash (Flash-Next) · prefill and decode on the R9700</h2>
        <p>
          Change-level campaigns from the 0.4.3 release notes, not a cumulative release matrix: each row names its card,
          fixture and arms. Numbers from different sessions are not chained.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>change</th><th>hardware and fixture</th><th>result</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {flashNextRows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.change}</td>
                  <td><span class="muted">{r.hw}</span></td>
                  <td><strong>{r.result}</strong>{r.quality && <> <span class="muted">· quality: {r.quality}</span></>}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, “Headline numbers” rows 1–4 and Highlights. The 16,384-row
          chunk is the default on gfx1201 under compact scratch; for short prompts with long generations set
          <code>HIPFIRE_PREFILL_CHUNK_ROWS=4096</code>. With a 4096-row chunk the pass is limited by the PCIe expert stream
          and the W1–W4 wins mostly hide. Opt-outs: <code>HIPFIRE_MQ6_I8=0</code>,
          <code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_COMPACT_SCRATCH=0</code>.
        </p>

        <h3>Expert hot set (on by default on gfx1201)</h3>
        <p>
          <code>hipfire pull {flashNext.tag}</code> fetches a 213 KB decode-routing sidecar and the load keeps the most-used
          experts resident in VRAM (gfx1201 only). On real text it is worth <strong>{hotSet.mtpGainPct} MTP decode</strong>
          (fiction prose +{hotSet.mtpProse} %, glimmer coding prompt +{hotSet.mtpCoding} %) and {hotSet.arGainPct} plain AR,
          with no prefill loss (pp16384 +{hotSet.prefillPct} %) and byte-identical output. The copy-heavy code edit under MTP
          is the exception at {hotSet.mtpCodeEdit.toString().replace("-", "−")} %. Fixture: {hotSet.fixture}. Source:
          {" "}<a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, Highlights (<code>HOTSET-DEFAULT-CHECK.md</code>).
        </p>
        <p>
          The release notes also publish this earlier on/off table, measured <strong>before</strong> the forward-scratch
          diet, the W1–W4 waves, the compact-scratch default and the 16,384-row chunk, and not re-measured on the final
          build. Treat it as a direction, not a current figure.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead><tr><th>measured on one R9700, earlier build</th><th class="num">change</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {hotSet.earlier.map((r) => (
                <tr><td>{r.vs}</td><td class="num">{r.change}</td></tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          <code>HIPFIRE_QWEN4_EXPERT_HOTSET=0</code> restores whole-layer placement. A phase-aware budget is planned for 0.4.4.
          Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, “Flash-Next on a single R9700”.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="mtp-decode" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Native MTP decode · release-gate serve batteries</h2>
        <p>
          These are the serve batteries the 0.4.3.1 release gate ran on RC <code>{flashNextMtp.rc}</code>
          ({flashNextMtp.date}). They are correctness gates with speed read off the same run, not a benchmark matrix: greedy,
          thinking off, one run of five prompts, AR arm then MTP arm, transcripts byte-identical.
        </p>

        <h3>Flash-Next · {flashNextMtp.card}</h3>
        <p class="footnote" style="margin-top: 0;">{flashNextMtp.model}</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>turn</th><th class="num">tokens</th><th class="num">τ</th><th class="num">MTP decode</th><th class="num">AR decode</th><th class="num">MTP vs AR</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {flashNextMtp.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.turn}</td>
                  <td class="num">{r.tokens}</td>
                  <td class="num">{f2(r.tau)}</td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{f1(r.mtp)}</strong> tok/s</td>
                  <td class="num">{f1(r.ar)} tok/s</td>
                  <td class="num">{pctOf(r.ar, r.mtp)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          MTP speeds up the code and word-problem turns a lot and the low-τ turns barely or not at all; the AR floor did not
          retire on any turn. Source: <code>RC-GATES-0.4.3.1-RESULT.md</code> §5. The same battery on the 0.4.3 RCs
          (<code>RC-GATES-0.4.3-RESULT.md</code>) shows the same shape.
        </p>

        <h3>Qwen3.8-27B · {mtp27b.card}</h3>
        <p class="footnote" style="margin-top: 0;">{mtp27b.model}</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>turn</th><th class="num">τ</th><th class="num">MTP decode</th><th class="num">AR decode</th><th class="num">MTP vs AR</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {mtp27b.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.turn}</td>
                  <td class="num">{f2(r.tau)}</td>
                  <td class="num"><strong>{f1(r.mtp)}</strong> tok/s</td>
                  <td class="num">{f1(r.ar)} tok/s</td>
                  <td class="num">{pctOf(r.ar, r.mtp)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          <code>--spec mtp</code> on the 27B failed to load on v0.4.3 (the sealed MTP route plan missed the head closure) and is
          fixed in <a href={PATCH_URL}>0.4.3.1</a>. The greedy serve transcript is byte-identical between AR and MTP. As on
          0.4.2.1, MTP greedy output on the 27B can differ from plain decode at near-tied tokens (the batched verify
          differs from decode at the ULP level); the gate saw one committed-id divergence in three bench prompts
          (<code>prose</code>, at generated token 147). Source: <code>RC-GATES-0.4.3.1-RESULT.md</code> §3, v0.4.3.1 release notes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="halo" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Strix Halo (gfx1151 + XDNA2) · Flash-Next 16K prefill</h2>
        <p>
          Client TTFT on a {f0(halo16k.tokens)}-token prompt, NPU trunk on, 3 fresh processes × 3 requests, all-9 median, on
          head <code>{halo16k.head}</code> ({halo16k.date}, job {halo16k.job}; method of job j1745 with the TTM-pool
          drain fixed).
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>measurement</th><th class="num">tok/s</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="row-hf">
                <td>0.4.3 all-9 median <span class="muted">(gate {f0(halo16k.gate)}: missed by {Math.abs(halo16k.missPct)} %, accepted for 0.4.3)</span></td>
                <td class="num"><strong>{f1(halo16k.median)}</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>0.4.3 steady requests <span class="muted">(four of nine)</span></td>
                <td class="num">{halo16k.steadyRange}</td>
              </tr>
              {halo16k.samples.map((p, i) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><span class="muted">process {i + 1}, requests 1–3</span></td>
                  <td class="num">{p.map(f1).join(" / ")}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
              <tr>
                <td>v0.4.2 shipped tip <span class="muted">(from the <a href="/benchmarks/0.4.2#npu-headline">0.4.2 page</a>, a separate measurement)</span></td>
                <td class="num">{f0(halo16k.v042Shipped)}</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          The miss comes from slow first requests after the TTM-pool drain; steady requests match the earlier pass on the
          previous head. Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, Headline numbers and Known issues;
          <code>RC-GATES-0.4.3-RESULT.md</code> (a).
        </p>
        <p class="footnote">
          0.4.3.1 regression check ({halo16k.regression.date}, RC <code>{halo16k.regression.rc}</code>, warm kernel caches, 16.36K
          tokens, not the official gate): RC requests {halo16k.regression.rcRequests.map(f1).join(" / ")} tok/s against the
          v0.4.3 control's {halo16k.regression.controlRequests.map(f1).join(" / ")}. No Strix Halo change in the 0.4.3.1
          commits; load, prefill and generate match v0.4.3. Source: <code>RC-GATES-0.4.3.1-RESULT.md</code> §6.
        </p>

        <h3>Strix Halo decode and speculation (contributor measurements)</h3>
        <p>
          These figures are the contributor's own ({" "}<a href="https://github.com/fivetide">@fivetide</a>) and were not
          re-measured on Halo hardware by the release gates.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead><tr><th>change</th><th>result</th><th>PR</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {haloContributor.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.change}</td>
                  <td><strong>{r.result}</strong></td>
                  <td><a href={`${REPO}/pull/${r.pr}`}>#{r.pr}</a></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, § Strix Halo.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="changes" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Other measured changes in 0.4.3</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead><tr><th>change</th><th>hardware and fixture</th><th>result</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {otherRows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{r.change}</td>
                  <td><span class="muted">{r.hw}</span></td>
                  <td><strong>{r.result}</strong></td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, Highlights, § Strix Halo, § 7900 XTX and narrow PCIe links,
          Fixes. The 7900 XTX narrow-link rows have one hold of evidence.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="tiers" class="bench-section">
        <h2>New Flash-Next tiers: mq3-xts and mq2-xts</h2>
        <p>
          Smaller split-base Flash-Next files than the 125 GB <code>mq4-xts</code>. They load their per-token embedding rows
          from a shared PLE sidecar (q4 29.1 GB, q8 54.4 GB). These tiers lose some quality against the default
          <code>mq4-xts</code>; measured KLD per tier is on the model cards.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="perf">
            <thead><tr><th>tag</th><th class="num">base size</th><th class="num">download, q4 / q8 PLE</th><th>what it is</th><th>GPUs</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {tiers.map((t) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{t.tag}</code></td>
                  <td class="num">{t.base}</td>
                  <td class="num">{t.dl}</td>
                  <td>{t.what}</td>
                  <td>{t.gpus}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="footnote">
          Example from the model card: {tierKld.tier} decode on a {tierKld.gpu} measures KLD {tierKld.kld} on
          {" "}{tierKld.corpus}. KLD numbers are not comparable across tiers or against MQ4 without the card's scope notes.
          Strix Halo refuses the mq2 tiers cleanly in this release. Model cards:
          <a href={flashNext.hf}>hipfire-models/qwen3.8-flash-next</a>. Source:
          <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3 release notes</a>, “New Flash-Next model tiers”.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="repro" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Reproduce it</h2>
        <pre class="code"><span class="cmt"># install hipfire (latest release), or pin one</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> curl -L {RELEASE_INSTALL_SCRIPT} | bash
<span class="prompt">$</span> curl -L {RELEASE_INSTALL_SCRIPT} | bash -s -- --tag {PATCH_TAG}

<span class="cmt"># A3B prefill and decode on a gfx1201 card (R9700)</span>
<span class="prompt">$</span> hipfire pull {a3bFile.mq4rsTag}
<span class="prompt">$</span> hipfire bench {a3bFile.mq4rsTag} --matrix --json --pp 512,1024,4096,8192,16384 \
    --tg 1 --ctx 1 --runs 3 --warmups 1 --spec off
<span class="prompt">$</span> hipfire bench {a3bFile.mq4rsTag} --spec off --matrix --pp 128 --ctx 128,8192 \
    --tg 128 --runs 3 --warmups 2</pre>
        <p class="footnote">
          Each version's default config applies; the A3B prefill comparison used an empty per-version home, so no user
          <code>config.toml</code> was read. Releases: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>{RELEASE_TAG}</a> (<a href={RELEASE_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG</a>),
          <a href={PATCH_URL}>{PATCH_TAG}</a> (<a href={PATCH_CHANGELOG}>CHANGELOG</a>). Protocol:
          <a href={`${REPO}/blob/${RELEASE_TAG}/docs/BENCHMARKS.md`}>BENCHMARKS.md</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="limits" class="bench-section">
        <h2>Known limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>The A3B speed-up applies to files built with the symmetric expert recipe, which is <code>{a3bFile.mq4rsTag}</code>. Fast mq4rs prefill is gfx1201 only, its router and lm_head are 4-bit, and <code>--spec mtp</code> is opt-in there (the default stays AR).</li>
          <li>On the asymmetric <code>{a3bFile.mq4rTag}</code> file, 0.4.3 prefill numerics change slightly and greedy output can differ from 0.4.2 at near-ties. Users on the old mq4r should pull mq4rs.</li>
          <li>Flash-Next on the R9700 prefills faster and decodes 5–10 % slower by default (16,384-row chunk); the earlier hot-set on/off table predates the final defaults.</li>
          <li>The Strix Halo 16K gate was missed at 2,365.6 against 2,386 tok/s and accepted for 0.4.3. Each process exit on Strix Halo leaves about 80 GiB in the kernel TTM page pool, so a back-to-back reload can be refused; the fix is planned for 0.4.4.</li>
          <li>Several Strix Halo and 7900 XTX results are the contributors' own measurements; the release gates had no Halo hardware for those PRs.</li>
          <li>The PM4 replay decode speed-up does not apply to other GPUs, to MTP-loaded requests or to a drafter.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="footnote">
          Source: <a href={RELEASE_URL}>v0.4.3</a> and <a href={PATCH_URL}>v0.4.3.1</a> release notes, Known issues and Upgrade notes.
        </p>
      </section>
